          "session_id": "full-g-md-015",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My knees hurt most days and I have been told to lose weight. Is joint pain ever urgent, and what does guidance describe as core treatment?",
          "answer": "**Is it urgent?** The symptom check rated your description as routine and found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. Long-standing knee pain that is not getting worse is usually handled with a planned appointment. Joint pain can be urgent, though. The osteoarthritis summary says a joint that is hot, swollen and acutely painful, especially with fever, suggests infection or crystal arthritis and needs urgent assessment. Seek prompt care for sudden or marked swelling, redness or warmth with fever, a joint that locks or gives way, or an injury or inability to bear weight. The sources I had do not cover weight-bearing or injury, so get those checked promptly rather than waiting. Sources also say chest pain, trouble breathing, or a swollen, painful calf need emergency care.\n\n**Core treatment.** Guidance on knee osteoarthritis treats structured exercise as the foundation, with the strongest and most consistent evidence of any intervention and a better safety profile than medicines. It combines strengthening the muscles around the knee, improving movement, and aerobic activity, usually supervised at first. Some discomfort within a comfortable range during exercise is not a sign of damage. Low-impact options such as water exercise, cycling and walking are described as well tolerated. During a flare, lower intensity for a few days is advised rather than stopping altogether. Education, pacing, footwear, walking aids, and heat or cold can also help.\n\n**Medicines and weight.** Medicines are add-ons to exercise, not replacements. Topical anti-inflammatory creams are the preferred first medicine where the joint is accessible. Joint replacement is considered only when pain and limitation persist despite non-surgical care. Opioids and glucosamine or chondroitin are not recommended. The sources disagree on weight. One osteoarthritis summary says weight management is part of care where relevant. The guidance I checked did not describe weight loss as a knee treatment, and the weight passages I found concerned blood pressure and diabetes. Ask a clinician how weight change might apply to your knees. A clinician can also examine your knees and advise on any medicines.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 17162.636874000098,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
          "red_flag_negation_suppressed": false,
